{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"advisory":[{"agreement":1,"block_threshold":0.95,"blocking_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Decide how _lifecycle_index should merge multiple lifecycle notes for one canonical key instead of silently keeping only the first (setdefault).","criteria":["correctness","edge-cases","maintainability"],"decision":"advisory","evidence":["`index.setdefault(replacement, f\"active — supersedes alias `{name}` ({status})\")`","`index.setdefault(replacement, f\"active — replaces removed key `{name}`\")`","Both loops write into the same `index` dict keyed by `replacement`; a canonical key targeted by both a deprecation and a tombstone (or two tombstones) keeps only the first note. Current registry has distinct replacement keys so it is not yet triggered, but nothing guards against future collisions."],"finding":"_lifecycle_index() uses setdefault when mapping a canonical key to its lifecycle note, so if the same canonical replacement key is the target of more than one deprecation alias and/or tombstone, only the first-encountered note survives and the others are silently dropped from the generated doc, with no warning or test guard.","id":"0","impact":0.3,"location":"scripts/gen_config_reference.py","norm_id":"nc519c48430e130ad","priority":0.3,"reason":"default-advisory","scenarios":["A future canonical key becomes the replacement for both a deprecated alias and a removed key, or for two removed keys."],"severity":"minor","suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":1.0,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"no","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impa